AB  - [RÉSUMÉ CLINIQUE ISCHIO - FR] Sur 96 lésions aiguës des ischio-jambiers explorées par IRM en NCAA Division I, un contact œdémateux avec le nerf sciatique est observé dans 78,1 % des cas (36,5 % partiel, 41,7 % circonférentiel complet) et corrèle directement avec un délai de retour au sport prolongé. | Points clés : L'œdème entre en contact avec le nerf sciatique dans 78,1 % des lésions aiguës (dont 41,7 % d'engainement complet). ; Corrélation significative entre le grade BAMIC, l'engainement sciatique complet et le nombre de jours d'arrêt sportif. ; Justifie l'intégration d'exercices de mobilisation neurodynamique douce du nerf sciatique durant la rééducation. | Pertinence Clinique : Étude prospective (BJSM 2026) montrant que l'engainement œdémateux circonférentiel du nerf sciatique en IRM initiale est présent dans 41,7 % des lésions aiguës chez le joueur de football américain et retarde significativement le retour au jeu. || [ABSTRACT ORIGINAL - EN] Objective To assess if the British Athletics Muscle Injury Classification (BAMIC) grade and sciatic nerve involvement on MRI at time of hamstring strain injury (HSI) were associated with the time to return-to-sport (RTS) in National Collegiate Athletic Association Division I American football players. Materials and methods Data were collected via a larger prospective cohort investigation of collegiate football players who sustained an HSI. MRIs at time of injury were assessed by a musculoskeletal radiologist using BAMIC. Sciatic nerve involvement was characterised by extent of adjacent oedema on MRI as no contact, partial (contacted by oedema) or complete (circumferentially surrounded by oedema). Spearman rank correlations, mixed-effects modelling and χ 2 analyses were used to determine associations between imaging characteristics, HSI severity and days missed before RTS. Results 96 unique HSI were included (100% male, mean age 20±1.58 years). Increasing BAMIC grade was associated with longer time to RTS (r=0.29, p=0.004). Sciatic nerve involvement was observed in 75/96 (78.1%) cases (35/96 (36.5%) partial, 40/96 (41.7%) complete), and no sciatic nerve involvement in 21/96 (21.9%) cases. There was a significant positive association between sciatic nerve involvement as BAMIC grade increased (p Conclusions Increasing radiologic severity, indicated by BAMIC grade and complete sciatic nerve involvement were associated with longer RTS following HSI. Sciatic nerve involvement on MRI should be considered in the assessment of acute HSI.
