AB  - [RÉSUMÉ CLINIQUE ISCHIO - FR] Mise au point exhaustive sur la sémiologie IRM des lésions des ischio-jambiers chez l'athlète : topographie exacte (BFlh, ST, SM), atteinte de la jonction myo-tendineuse ou du tendon central, avulsions proximales et critères pronostiques de rechute. | Points clés : Deux mécanismes distincts : sprint (chef long du biceps fémoral) vs étirement extrême (semi-membraneux proximal). ; Nécessité d'un langage standardisé entre radiologue et chirurgien (intégrité du tendon central, perte de tension, gap). ; Identification précoce des lésions à haut risque chirurgical (avulsions tubérositaires et ruptures du tendon libre). | Pertinence Clinique : Synthèse radiologique complète 2026 (*Skeletal Radiology*) standardisant le compte-rendu IRM entre radiologues ostéo-articulaires, médecins du sport et chirurgiens orthopédistes. || [ABSTRACT ORIGINAL - EN] The hamstring muscles comprise the semimembranosus, semitendinosus, and biceps femoris. The hamstring muscle group is frequently injured in a wide range of sports, particularly those involving sprinting, rapid acceleration and deceleration, and changes of direction. The most common mechanism is an indirect injury, typically presenting as a muscle strain. In elite athletes, hamstring injuries can be career-threatening. Complete hamstring avulsions are less common than partial tears. This review provides an overview of the broad spectrum of sports-related hamstring injuries and their magnetic resonance imaging findings. Furthermore, both classic and more recent classification systems for muscle injuries are presented and discussed, as several systems have been proposed and currently no universally accepted standard exists. In addition, basic principles of treatment, the healing process of muscle injuries, and the risk of re-injury are outlined. Factors influencing the time to return to play are also discussed. Since radiologists play a key role in the multidisciplinary care of both athletes and the general population with muscle injuries, communication with referring physicians should be as standardized as possible to have the same language and based on clinically aligned terminology.
