AB  - [RÉSUMÉ CLINIQUE ISCHIO - FR] Suivi à moyen/long terme (6,2 à 10,6 ans, moyenne 7,6 ans) de réparations endoscopiques proximales des ischio-jambiers : score HOS-Sports moyen de 89,2/100, EVA douleur à 1,7/10, 86,4 % de satisfaction et 85,7 % des patients atteignant le seuil PASS. | Points clés : Plus long suivi publié (moyenne 7,6 ans, jusqu'à 10,6 ans) pour la réparation endoscopique proximale. ; 86,4 % de satisfaction globale et 85,7 % des patients atteignant un état symptomatique acceptable (PASS). ; Indication privilégiée : ruptures partielles de haut grade, tendinopathies fissuraires et avulsions aiguës peu rétractées (< 2–3 cm). | Pertinence Clinique : Confirme la durabilité à long terme (suivi moyen 7,6 ans, min 6,2 ans) de la réparation endoscopique pour les lésions partielles ou complètes peu rétractées. || [ABSTRACT ORIGINAL - EN] Purpose To evaluate midterm outcomes of endoscopic proximal hamstring repair in a previously established cohort. Methods A retrospective study design was used for a midterm (minimum 6 years) follow-up time. Patients completed the questionnaires over the phone or electronically on REDCap after consent. The questionnaires included the modified Harris Hip Score, the Hip Outcome (HOS), the Sports Subscale (HOS-SSS), the Single Assessment Numeric Evaluation, the International Hip Outcome Tool-12, VAS pain scale, and queried on satisfaction with the surgery. Descriptive statistics including mean and variance were used to analyze the results and compare values preoperatively and at the midterm time point. Results Twenty two of 29 individuals participated (75.8%). Minimum follow-up time was 6.2 years with a maximum follow-up time of 10.6 years (mean 7.6, SD 1.08). Subjects reported a mean modified Harris Hip Score of 82.6 (SD 17.1, range 46.2-100.1), mean HOS of 83.2 (SD 19.9, range 33.8-100), mean HOS-SSS of 89.2 (SD 15, range 50-100, n = 12), mean Single Assessment Numeric Evaluation of 80.2% (SD 20.6, range 30-100%), mean the International Hip Outcome Tool-12 of 79.5 (SD 22.7, range 32.5-100), mean pain score of 1.7 (SD 2.4, range 0-7), and 86.4% satisfaction with the surgery. Conclusions Endoscopic hamstring repair remains beneficial with high patient satisfaction (86.4%, n = 22) and patient-reported outcome scores (85.7% reaching acceptable symptom state, n = 21) at midterm follow-up. Level of evidence Level IV, retrospective case series.
