AB  - [RÉSUMÉ CLINIQUE ISCHIO - FR] Comparaison appariée de 32 révisions chirurgicales et 128 réparations primaires (recul ~4 ans). Si le score PHAS médian atteint 85,0 après révision (vs 91,5 en primaire, p = 0,01), le risque de nouvelle rupture est multiplié par 8 (31,3 % vs 3,9 % ; HR = 8,19). | Points clés : Nécessité d'une allogreffe dans 37,5 % des chirurgies de révision (contre 9,4 % en primaire). ; Score PHAS médian honorable après révision (85,0 vs 91,5 en primaire). ; Risque de re-rupture multiplié par 8 en révision (31,3 % vs 3,9 %, HR = 8,19). ; Souligne l'importance capitale de réussir la réparation primaire en phase aiguë (< 32 jours). | Pertinence Clinique : Étude cas-témoins appariée 1:4 (32 révisions vs 128 primaires) quantifiant le défi technique des reprises chirurgicales : recours à une allogreffe dans 37,5 % des cas et risque de re-rupture itérative de 31,3 %. || [ABSTRACT ORIGINAL - EN] Background Proximal hamstring avulsion injury (PHAI) repairs are increasing with the growing frequency of this pathology. That is why rerupture is increasingly encountered with rare studies reporting this outcome. Purpose To compare the functional outcomes of revision versus primary PHAI surgery as measured by the Parisian Hamstring Avulsion Score (PHAS) and to compare other outcomes scores, return-to-sport status, and complication rates. Study design Case-control study; Level of evidence, 4. Methods This single-center, matched case-control study was conducted from 2009 to 2023 at a center specializing in sports surgery. It included adults >18 years of age who underwent revision or primary PHAI repair and had a minimum 1-year follow-up. The study involved matching patients who underwent revision surgery to patients who underwent primary surgery at a 1:4 ratio based on age, sex, Tegner Activity Scale (TAS) score before injury, type of rupture, and follow-up duration. Surgical treatment was indicated for complete PHAI, partial tendon avulsion with >2 cm of retraction, or nonresponse to 6 months of nonoperative treatment. Outcomes measured were the PHAS, complication rate including rerupture, TAS score, UCLA activity score, and patient satisfaction. Results The study included 160 patients, with 128 in the primary group and 32 in the revision group. The mean age for the primary group was 48.6 years (SD, 9.4 years), and for the revision group, it was 48.8 years (SD, 9.4 years); both groups had similar follow-up periods: 45.5 months (SD, 39.5 months) for the primary group and 51.0 months (SD, 46.5 months) for the revision group. Allograft usage was substantially higher in the revision group at 37.5% (12/32) compared with 9.4% (12/128) in the primary group ( P = .0003). At the final follow-up, PHAS values were lower in the revision group with a median of 85.0 (IQR, 64.5-92.0) versus 91.5 (IQR, 78.5-97.0) in the primary group ( P = .01). The rerupture rate also differed significantly, standing at 31.3% (10/32) in the revision group compared with only 3.9% (5/128) in the primary group, with Kaplan-Meier and Cox regression analyses confirming a higher risk of rerupture, quantified as 8.19 (95% CI, 2.76-24.3; P P = .02). TAS and UCLA scores did not show significant differences ( P > .05). Rates of return to sport were similarly comparable across the groups ( P > .05). Pain reported as none or mild affected 72.4% (89/123) of the primary group, but only 50.0% (11/22) of the revision group, marking a significant difference ( P = .01). Conclusion Revision PHAI repair is associated with lower functional scores and nearly 8 times the risk of rerupture compared with primary surgery. Furthermore, it carries a higher rate of complications.
